---
// Lesson 4.5 — The Full State Machine: Back to Patrol
// The capstone of Unit 4: patrol → chase → attack → return, drawn.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.5 — The Full State Machine: Back to Patrol</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Everything you built this unit is one animal: a machine with
    states, transitions, and no memory it does not need. Today you draw
    it, trace it, and connect it to the biggest idea in the AP exam's
    algorithms unit: selection decides everything.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        You have written every line of this enemy already. Read the
        whole Update path at once and count the behaviors:
    </p>
    <pre><code>private void Update()
{
    if (isPlatformer)
    {
        PlatformerMove();   // STATE: PATROL
    }
    else
    {
        TopDownMove();      // STATE: PATROL (when far) or CHASE (when near)
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Inside TopDownMove the chase is an if; outside it, the attack
        is a collision handler with a cooldown. Now draw the machine
        on paper — states as boxes, transitions as labeled arrows:
    </p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>From</th><th>Event / condition</th><th>To</th></tr>
        <tr><td>PATROL</td><td>distance to player &lt; chaseDistance</td><td>CHASE</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CHASE</td><td>every frame: move toward player (normalized)</td><td>(stay in CHASE)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CHASE</td><td>contact (collision)</td><td>ATTACK (deals damage on cooldown)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CHASE</td><td>distance &gt;= chaseDistance</td><td>PATROL</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        The fourth row is the one the teacher's design asks about:
        <strong>"returns to patrol when the player flees."</strong>
        Find it in the code — there is no explicit "return to patrol"
        line. The chase is inside an if; when the if stops matching,
        the enemy simply falls back to doing nothing extra. The
        patrol was never switched off, it was just outvoted. The most
        elegant state machines hide exits inside conditions.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The AP exam says an algorithm needs <strong>sequencing,
            selection, and iteration</strong>. Label all three in the
            enemy's code: where is sequence, where is selection, and
            where is iteration? (The iteration one is sneaky — think
            about what calls Update.)
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — prove the diagram with Debug.Log.</strong>
    Instrument TopDownMove for five minutes (then delete it):</p>
    <pre><code>if (dist &lt; chaseDistance)
{
    Debug.Log("CHASE");
    ...
}
else
{
    Debug.Log("PATROL");  // you will have to make the else explicit
}</code></pre>

    <p>Walk the boundary in and out: the Console flickers
    CHASE/PATROL/CHASE. You are watching state transitions print in
    real time. Why is "flickering at the edge" not a bug — and which
    number (chaseDistance) would you tune to make the flicker stop?</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the ATTACK column is a lie (mostly).</strong>
    Notice: attack is not a state in the code, it is a collision
    event that can fire during CHASE or even during PATROL (walk into
    a patrolling enemy!). Your diagram probably drew it as a state
    anyway — because that is how it FEELS. Designers draw the
    machine from the player's point of view; the code just makes the
    arrows cheap. Bosses in Unit 8 will have real phase states —
    this enemy fakes them.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — name the states out loud.</strong> Write the
    three names — PATROL, CHASE, ATTACK — as comments next to the
    code lines that implement them. The comment IS the design
    document; the code is one implementation of it.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Break the machine</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Swap the chaseDistance and patrolDistance values (5
                and 3). Which behavior gets weird first — and does
                the diagram still hold?</li>
            <li>Make isPlatformer true in the Overworld. Which states
                vanish — and what does that say about the enemy's
                whole brain in the cave scenes?</li>
            <li>Add a second chase if INSIDE the first: chase when
                dist &lt; 5 AND dist &gt; 2. What did you just invent
                (a dead zone!) — and where does the enemy stand when
                the player stands still inside it?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Design a fourth state</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Invent FLEE: the enemy runs AWAY when you get within
                1.5 (a cowardly guard). Which subtraction flips, and
                where does the new arrow go on your diagram?</li>
            <li>Invent ENRAGE: after taking a hit (someday), double
                speed for 3 seconds. What field counts the 3 seconds
                — and which pattern does it borrow?</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"4.5a: state diagram + states named"</code> — include a photo or ASCII of your diagram in the repo.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Draw the enemy's state machine from memory, with conditions on the arrows</li>
            <li>Find "return to patrol" in the code — as the absence of a match</li>
            <li>Label sequencing, selection, and iteration in real code</li>
            <li>Explain why a drawn state and a coded state are different things</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/enemy-state-machines.html">Enemy State Machines</a> — the full reference, with the boss preview</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/index.html">Unit 4: The Enemy</a> — every branch annotated</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/reference-sheet.html">Exam Reference Sheet</a> — where selection meets pseudocode</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="5.1.html">5.1 — The Axe: Item Pickups &amp; Inventory Bools</a> — Unit 5 begins.</p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="4.5" title="4.5a The Full State Machine: Back to Patrol — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
